1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:01,460
My name is Fuad Laguda.

2
00:00:01,531 --> 00:00:06,783
I am an independent engineer in Stirling,
Scotland, and this is One Flag, No Deploy.

3
00:00:07,251 --> 00:00:10,096
We have like 20 minutes,
so this is going to move.

4
00:00:10,479 --> 00:00:12,110
Here is the idea behind this talk.

5
00:00:12,389 --> 00:00:16,258
If you've never forced your backup
to run, you have never seen it work.

6
00:00:16,541 --> 00:00:20,011
I had a backup like that from
the day my app went live.

7
00:00:20,293 --> 00:00:24,093
It had a backup writer that
nobody has ever seen take over.

8
00:00:24,389 --> 00:00:25,729
I have never seen take over.

9
00:00:26,041 --> 00:00:27,549
So I made it take over.

10
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:32,896
On 24th of September, I pulled the
plug on my production app on purpose.

11
00:00:33,230 --> 00:00:37,851
I recorded it and I will show you the
diagnostic in the middle of this talk.

12
00:00:38,804 --> 00:00:40,505
First, the honest scale.

13
00:00:40,965 --> 00:00:44,184
So nobody thinks this is
a thousand-node story.

14
00:00:44,581 --> 00:00:49,282
I run two production and web applications
and, I'm the only engineer behind both.

15
00:00:49,483 --> 00:00:50,288
There is no team.

16
00:00:50,305 --> 00:00:55,006
So if something breaks at 11:00
at night, the person who needs

17
00:00:55,006 --> 00:00:56,976
to get up and fix it is me.

18
00:00:57,050 --> 00:01:00,150
The person who needs to wake
up at night and worry about how

19
00:01:00,155 --> 00:01:02,007
long the downtime is, is me.

20
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:04,334
Both started as tools for friends.

21
00:01:04,357 --> 00:01:08,351
Postbarrel helps people turn their
stories into social media video

22
00:01:08,354 --> 00:01:12,369
scripts, and Programcard helps
trainers manage their clients.

23
00:01:12,415 --> 00:01:17,890
Postbarrel now has plain-paying customers,
and that is why I need to be very

24
00:01:17,892 --> 00:01:20,181
careful with the way the failover works.

25
00:01:20,988 --> 00:01:23,433
Here is the constraint
that everything comes from.

26
00:01:23,779 --> 00:01:28,467
When something goes wrong in
production, I cannot ship a build

27
00:01:28,523 --> 00:01:31,432
with more logging in it and wait.

28
00:01:31,740 --> 00:01:33,253
A deploy is minutes.

29
00:01:33,427 --> 00:01:36,067
Minutes is fine on a Tuesday afternoon.

30
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:40,842
It is not fine when a user is sitting
there, with a spinner and I'm trying

31
00:01:40,842 --> 00:01:43,157
to work out why something is wrong.

32
00:01:43,645 --> 00:01:48,990
And I cannot leave the logging on all
the time either because the detailed

33
00:01:49,054 --> 00:01:51,577
trace holds the user's own answers.

34
00:01:51,618 --> 00:01:56,618
In Postbarrel, the app I tested this
on, the trace carries the user script.

35
00:01:56,678 --> 00:02:01,954
That is personal data sitting at rest in
a store and leaving it there because it

36
00:02:01,954 --> 00:02:04,523
might be useful one day is not a decision.

37
00:02:04,589 --> 00:02:08,288
It's laziness with somebody
else's personal data.

38
00:02:09,068 --> 00:02:15,322
So I needed the diagnostic to be something
I could raise and lower in seconds from

39
00:02:15,322 --> 00:02:17,130
a laptop without shipping anything.

40
00:02:17,260 --> 00:02:18,663
That is a flag.

41
00:02:18,866 --> 00:02:20,349
It is not a feature flag.

42
00:02:20,606 --> 00:02:26,639
nobody is being shown a new button, but
it is the same mechanism pointed at a

43
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:32,605
different problem and that turns out to
be the most of what I want to say today

44
00:02:34,341 --> 00:02:39,870
The switch is one key in Cloudflare
Workers KV, which is where my app run.

45
00:02:40,299 --> 00:02:42,399
The key is called traceMode.

46
00:02:42,565 --> 00:02:46,076
It has three states: off, admin, all.

47
00:02:46,606 --> 00:02:51,400
Off is the default, and off is also
what you get if the key is missing,

48
00:02:51,453 --> 00:02:56,361
or if it holds something I do not
recognize, or if the read fails.

49
00:02:56,392 --> 00:03:01,027
I will come back to that last one
because it matters more than it sounds.

50
00:03:01,407 --> 00:03:07,627
Admin means full trace are stored,
but only for my own admin account.

51
00:03:07,873 --> 00:03:12,608
All means full traces are stored
for every generation by every user,

52
00:03:12,633 --> 00:03:15,764
which is the one I almost never use.

53
00:03:15,999 --> 00:03:21,551
So every request reads that key, and
it is not just once at the startup

54
00:03:21,610 --> 00:03:23,723
or cached for like five minutes.

55
00:03:24,096 --> 00:03:29,226
Every request reads it because a flag
that is read at startup is not a switch,

56
00:03:29,247 --> 00:03:32,154
it is just a deploy with extra steps.

57
00:03:32,334 --> 00:03:37,207
Now, people flinch at that, you
see, because reading a key on every

58
00:03:37,208 --> 00:03:41,958
request sounds like you are adding
a network call to your hot path.

59
00:03:42,234 --> 00:03:44,501
But that is what KV is built for.

60
00:03:44,759 --> 00:03:49,625
It is designed for high volume
reads and frequently read values are

61
00:03:49,634 --> 00:03:51,840
cached across Cloudflare network.

62
00:03:52,065 --> 00:03:57,993
A hot read can be served from the cache
rather than going all the way back

63
00:03:58,012 --> 00:03:59,948
to the central storage every time.

64
00:04:00,174 --> 00:04:05,827
My request takes seconds because there
is, a language model at the end of it, so

65
00:04:05,828 --> 00:04:09,668
the KV read is negligible by comparison.

66
00:04:10,416 --> 00:04:12,303
Now, there is a trade-off though.

67
00:04:12,794 --> 00:04:15,611
KV is eventually consistent.

68
00:04:15,986 --> 00:04:19,382
If I change the flag, another
location can temporarily see the

69
00:04:19,406 --> 00:04:21,534
previous value because of caching.

70
00:04:22,105 --> 00:04:27,092
Cloudflare says changes can take
up to sixty seconds or more to

71
00:04:27,092 --> 00:04:31,450
become visible in other locations,
depending on cached values.

72
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:34,542
That is a trade-off I can live with.

73
00:04:34,542 --> 00:04:39,739
I, I would rather have a cheap, highly
readable configuration switch with

74
00:04:39,739 --> 00:04:44,990
some propagation delay than turn a
debugging flag into another deployment.

75
00:04:45,435 --> 00:04:50,003
So back to the why three
states and not a Boolean.

76
00:04:50,280 --> 00:04:57,278
A Boolean gives you two bad options:
logging off- Which is, where you live

77
00:04:57,462 --> 00:05:02,290
and logging on for everybody, which is
where you go when you are desperate.

78
00:05:02,593 --> 00:05:06,105
Turning it on for everybody in the
middle of an incident means you

79
00:05:06,111 --> 00:05:11,804
start capturing every user's private
content in order to debug one person's

80
00:05:11,809 --> 00:05:16,177
problem, which can be a problem in
its own if you forget to turn it off.

81
00:05:16,211 --> 00:05:20,342
Imagine every user's private
writing sitting in logs.

82
00:05:20,605 --> 00:05:23,145
Now, the third state removes that choice.

83
00:05:23,208 --> 00:05:27,851
Admin means I can reproduce the problem
on my own account with everything

84
00:05:27,851 --> 00:05:30,907
recorded and no other user is touched.

85
00:05:31,588 --> 00:05:36,427
Two more details that took me longer
to arrive at than they should have is

86
00:05:36,473 --> 00:05:39,038
that a read failure is treated as off.

87
00:05:39,305 --> 00:05:43,887
If KV is unreachable, the code does
not throw and it does not retry.

88
00:05:44,369 --> 00:05:48,114
It logs one line and carries
on as if the switch were off.

89
00:05:48,322 --> 00:05:53,574
In my view, that's okay because the
worst outcome of a flag failing should

90
00:05:53,584 --> 00:05:55,640
be that you lose the diagnostic.

91
00:05:55,807 --> 00:05:58,075
It should never be that
you lose the request.

92
00:05:58,407 --> 00:06:04,720
And the trace expire after 24 hours
because if accidentally I leave this

93
00:06:04,720 --> 00:06:08,722
switch on, I do not want the mistake
following me around for six months.

94
00:06:08,727 --> 00:06:10,072
I will forget one day.

95
00:06:10,087 --> 00:06:10,977
Everyone forgets.

96
00:06:11,008 --> 00:06:14,453
You could leave the switch on and six
months later you have a huge pile of

97
00:06:14,470 --> 00:06:15,963
logs that you don't even care about.

98
00:06:16,221 --> 00:06:18,781
So that's why I set
the expiry to 24 hours.

99
00:06:18,793 --> 00:06:22,702
So should there be any issue, I
know it will just cost me a day.

100
00:06:23,201 --> 00:06:26,882
One more thing, because people
get it wrong, not all logging

101
00:06:26,888 --> 00:06:28,871
belongs behind the switch.

102
00:06:29,352 --> 00:06:34,419
The cheap lines, which provider
answered, how many tokens that

103
00:06:34,430 --> 00:06:38,509
a retry happened carry no user
content, so they are always on.

104
00:06:38,788 --> 00:06:41,197
They are the first thing I
read when something breaks.

105
00:06:41,405 --> 00:06:45,210
The switch guards only the
private part, the prompts, the

106
00:06:45,215 --> 00:06:47,039
replies, the finished script.

107
00:06:47,167 --> 00:06:52,784
If your flag is hiding your operational
logs, it is on the wrong door.

108
00:06:53,528 --> 00:06:53,830
Okay?

109
00:06:55,247 --> 00:06:58,482
The admin state is the one I want
to spend more time on because

110
00:06:58,527 --> 00:07:00,564
it is the state I actually use.

111
00:07:00,744 --> 00:07:04,602
Here is the shape of a real
incident for one person.

112
00:07:05,075 --> 00:07:10,414
A user writes and says the output
was wrong, not broken, but wrong.

113
00:07:10,867 --> 00:07:14,643
There is no error or operational
log because the thing worked.

114
00:07:14,705 --> 00:07:16,390
The issue is the output.

115
00:07:16,427 --> 00:07:19,138
You cannot debug that from counters.

116
00:07:19,153 --> 00:07:22,284
You need to see what went
in and what came out.

117
00:07:22,294 --> 00:07:25,662
So switch to admin to reproduce it.

118
00:07:25,674 --> 00:07:28,775
Same input as best as I can get them.

119
00:07:28,780 --> 00:07:33,066
The full trace is written for
my generation and nobody else.

120
00:07:33,419 --> 00:07:35,849
Read it, find it, switch it back off.

121
00:07:35,888 --> 00:07:41,564
The loop is minutes and at no point
did I look at anyone else's writing.

122
00:07:42,284 --> 00:07:47,599
What it costs is honesty about how--
what the admin can actually do.

123
00:07:47,670 --> 00:07:50,631
It only helps when I can
reproduce the problem.

124
00:07:50,854 --> 00:07:55,501
If the fault is specific to that
user's data, admin is of no use,

125
00:07:55,845 --> 00:08:00,741
and the only thing that would help
is setting everything to all, which

126
00:08:00,763 --> 00:08:05,585
records everyone's generation for as
long as it is on, not just theirs.

127
00:08:05,790 --> 00:08:08,662
That is why I have not needed it.

128
00:08:08,886 --> 00:08:11,250
If I ever do, it stays on for minutes.

129
00:08:11,316 --> 00:08:15,066
The twenty-four-hour expiry
and the notes in the file are

130
00:08:15,107 --> 00:08:17,227
guardrails and I would let them be.

131
00:08:17,742 --> 00:08:18,818
That is the trade.

132
00:08:18,919 --> 00:08:22,279
A third state does not
remove the hard case.

133
00:08:22,311 --> 00:08:26,857
It removes the easy case from the
hard pile, which is most of them.

134
00:08:27,859 --> 00:08:29,949
Same shape, different problem.

135
00:08:30,133 --> 00:08:35,108
The writer for this app is Claude
Sonnet and if Anthropic API is

136
00:08:35,113 --> 00:08:37,224
unavailable, nobody gets a script.

137
00:08:37,582 --> 00:08:41,400
That is the whole product
stopped because somebody else's

138
00:08:41,452 --> 00:08:43,294
API is having an afternoon.

139
00:08:43,634 --> 00:08:48,122
So there is a fallback and the
fallback is where the counter comes in.

140
00:08:48,943 --> 00:08:53,369
Here is the ladder A call to
the writer makes four attempts.

141
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:58,401
Between them, it waits one second,
three seconds, eight seconds, each

142
00:08:58,434 --> 00:09:03,827
with a quarter jitter either side so
that concurrent requests do not all

143
00:09:03,827 --> 00:09:06,963
hit a recovering API on the same beat.

144
00:09:07,235 --> 00:09:10,014
Each attempt has a hard timeout.

145
00:09:10,090 --> 00:09:13,968
Only when all four have failed
does it go anywhere else.

146
00:09:14,170 --> 00:09:16,088
So why four, not one?

147
00:09:16,113 --> 00:09:21,251
Because a failed API request does
not necessarily mean the API is down.

148
00:09:21,569 --> 00:09:25,259
Anthropic exposes different
failure conditions, including

149
00:09:25,427 --> 00:09:29,485
rate limiting, internal error
timeouts, and temporary overload.

150
00:09:29,784 --> 00:09:34,584
Some of those are transient, which
means immediately abandoning the primary

151
00:09:34,586 --> 00:09:39,698
provider can send perfectly recoverable
failures straight to the fallback.

152
00:09:40,135 --> 00:09:43,579
And there is a case that is
deliberately never retried.

153
00:09:43,887 --> 00:09:49,524
A definite 400, a bad request that
is a bug or configuration fault

154
00:09:49,586 --> 00:09:54,858
on my side and burning 12 seconds
of retries on it helps nobody.

155
00:09:54,908 --> 00:09:56,370
It throws immediately.

156
00:09:56,490 --> 00:10:00,617
That exception matters more than it
looks, and I will come back to it.

157
00:10:01,375 --> 00:10:05,446
When the four attempts are gone, the
same request is translated and sent to

158
00:10:05,486 --> 00:10:10,389
Qwen on Nubius, a different model, a
different company on a different cloud.

159
00:10:10,462 --> 00:10:11,966
That separation matters.

160
00:10:12,048 --> 00:10:16,986
If your fallback is another region of
the same provider, you have built a

161
00:10:16,986 --> 00:10:23,168
fallback for a data center problem,
not necessarily a provider-wide outage

162
00:10:25,069 --> 00:10:30,035
Now, this choice was not read off a
leaderboard or anything like that.

163
00:10:30,091 --> 00:10:33,785
I sent the same captured
prompts to five candidates.

164
00:10:34,168 --> 00:10:37,750
Qwen came back the strongest
for the combination of speed

165
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:39,182
and cost in my testing.

166
00:10:39,209 --> 00:10:44,728
So the prompts are tuned for Sonnet and
tuned separately for Qwen on Nubeos to

167
00:10:44,728 --> 00:10:47,691
get as close as I can to the same result.

168
00:10:49,218 --> 00:10:53,752
Qwen is not as good as Sonnet,
and I do not pretend otherwise.

169
00:10:53,793 --> 00:10:57,419
It parrots example
phrases out of the prompt.

170
00:10:57,441 --> 00:10:59,464
It sharpens loose numbers.

171
00:10:59,524 --> 00:11:04,776
Its endings are soft, and knowing
exactly how it is worse is the

172
00:11:04,776 --> 00:11:06,765
point of having it measured.

173
00:11:07,049 --> 00:11:09,332
Two more things the flag
does is that it tells me.

174
00:11:09,752 --> 00:11:13,603
It hands off writes a line naming
the provider, the model, the tokens,

175
00:11:13,632 --> 00:11:15,193
and why the primary one failed.

176
00:11:15,449 --> 00:11:20,147
It also sends me one email throttled
to one an hour, so an outage

177
00:11:20,168 --> 00:11:22,439
does not become an inbox event.

178
00:11:22,736 --> 00:11:26,218
And the response is tagged with
which provider served it, so

179
00:11:26,489 --> 00:11:30,791
it shows up in the cost report
rather than just being a mystery.

180
00:11:31,093 --> 00:11:32,054
And there is a ping.

181
00:11:32,511 --> 00:11:36,970
Every night at 3:00, the app
sends about 40 tokens through the

182
00:11:37,204 --> 00:11:39,422
exact production failover path.

183
00:11:39,531 --> 00:11:42,113
Same transition, same URL, same model.

184
00:11:42,233 --> 00:11:45,781
It costs less than a penny a year,
and it emails me if it fails.

185
00:11:45,791 --> 00:11:51,235
So I'm not always completely in the dark
about how each of my model is running.

186
00:11:51,550 --> 00:11:55,166
But a 40 token ping is not a script.

187
00:11:55,166 --> 00:11:58,861
It doesn't prove that the app
itself is functioning well.

188
00:11:59,074 --> 00:12:02,835
What it does prove is that the door is
open, but it doesn't prove that there's

189
00:12:02,880 --> 00:12:08,296
anything on the other side So it is
time to do a real production test.

190
00:12:08,557 --> 00:12:12,892
Now, I could not wait for Anthropic
to have a bad day, so I caused one.

191
00:12:13,259 --> 00:12:16,665
My question to me was:
What am I allowed to fake?

192
00:12:16,852 --> 00:12:20,332
My first idea was the obvious
one, put a bad API key.

193
00:12:20,752 --> 00:12:25,449
That does not work, and it fails in the
worst possible way, which is quietly.

194
00:12:25,742 --> 00:12:30,803
A bad key returns 401, and 401,
like 400, is on the never retry

195
00:12:30,825 --> 00:12:33,296
list I-- that I mentioned earlier.

196
00:12:33,574 --> 00:12:37,937
The code throws on the first attempt
and the failover is never reached.

197
00:12:38,274 --> 00:12:42,948
My second idea was to edit
the code, record and revert.

198
00:12:43,474 --> 00:12:49,447
Now, that is worse because then this
talk and the test would describe a

199
00:12:49,455 --> 00:12:54,565
system that does not exist at the
moment of the test or after it.

200
00:12:55,079 --> 00:12:58,091
So the best idea was to add a second key.

201
00:12:58,614 --> 00:13:03,598
Same store, same shape as the
trace flag off by default.

202
00:13:03,840 --> 00:13:06,314
Read the failure treated as off.

203
00:13:06,579 --> 00:13:11,524
When it is set, every attempt at
Anthropic is treated as an overload

204
00:13:11,611 --> 00:13:13,520
without the request being made.

205
00:13:13,850 --> 00:13:17,081
That is the only thing
that is faked, the outage.

206
00:13:17,318 --> 00:13:20,257
So everything after it will be real.

207
00:13:20,455 --> 00:13:26,292
The four attempts run, the, the wait
happens, the email goes, the transition

208
00:13:26,292 --> 00:13:31,645
runs, the Queued call is a real call
with real tokens and a real bill.

209
00:13:31,852 --> 00:13:35,981
The script comes back through
the real-- with real checks.

210
00:13:36,208 --> 00:13:42,635
the log line says "forced" in capital on
purpose because a log that cannot tell the

211
00:13:42,636 --> 00:13:47,975
difference between an outage you suffered
and one you caused is a log that will lie

212
00:13:47,975 --> 00:13:52,787
to somebody eventually one day, and that
will probably be me in, like, six months.

213
00:13:53,282 --> 00:13:56,314
So as you can see, everything here
at the moment right now is fine.

214
00:13:56,314 --> 00:14:00,045
So the first thing we're gonna do is we're
gonna put the flag on, and we're gonna

215
00:14:00,046 --> 00:14:02,853
trigger it, and let's wait for it to run.

216
00:14:03,703 --> 00:14:08,319
Here we go And that is the
60-second timer going on.

217
00:14:08,604 --> 00:14:12,218
So while the 60 second is going
on, we're gonna go to the site.

218
00:14:12,260 --> 00:14:14,262
There we go, postbarrel.com

219
00:14:15,039 --> 00:14:24,226
and we click on New, click on Storytime,
click on New Script, Continue.

220
00:14:24,242 --> 00:14:27,492
So I have a story here in Notepad.

221
00:14:27,497 --> 00:14:30,506
I'm just gonna copy and
I'm gonna paste it there.

222
00:14:31,204 --> 00:14:36,774
Then I'm going to click
on Write My Script.

223
00:14:37,415 --> 00:14:41,598
select a time, two and
then let's wait for it

224
00:14:42,598 --> 00:14:45,620
Now, you would not have noticed it,
but I've actually skipped forward.

225
00:14:45,725 --> 00:14:48,126
Now, if you didn't notice that I've
skipped forward, that means my video

226
00:14:48,128 --> 00:14:50,563
editing skills are, like, legendary.

227
00:14:50,965 --> 00:14:52,485
So there has been something here.

228
00:14:52,625 --> 00:14:56,268
It dragged longer than it should
be, but here is the script.

